        "verification": {
          "verified": false,
          "reason": "unknown_key",
          "signature": "-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niHUEABYKAB0WIQR0njH6YGwX8twR6o61sr4a9zZpowUCaqM6nAAKCRC1sr4a9zZp\no4TaAPwNUo925mMaVsCFbsE1ln3osIqyU9IOVn85+3ZF5O1gzwD/ddsAzbRGSYNQ\nQbDBPQ80tyLXgpTbZbl/wq9IOkkLWwY=\n=xb3V\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----",
          "payload": "tree d27819a5dc1a95c152e2fe3d273abff4e52d53ef\nparent 4ec2d5d2c28684d50fe43a6300ab4cc86c6e0e3f\nauthor Hartley McGuire <skipkayhil@gmail.com> 1789081365 -0400\ncommitter Hartley McGuire <skipkayhil@gmail.com> 1789082139 -0400\n\nMove timestamp_attributes_for into SchemaContext\n\nInstead of computing the values lazily (which doesn't play well with\nRactors), this now computes the values when the schema is loaded. Now,\nas long as the schema is loaded on the main Ractor, these values can be\nused on non-main Ractors.\n\nFor some code specific \"why\":\n- Hooking reload_from_schema_cache is no longer needed because when the\n  schema reloads, the SchemaContext object is thrown away (so there's\n  nothing to manually reset)\n- timestamp_attribute_for_{create,update} were made public because we\n  have to call them inside the SchemaContext now\n",
          "verified_at": null
        }
